# Punar periodic shadow-AI detection (milestone-10.md §3.1/§3.2, decisions
# 1 and 2; SPEC sections 23, 6.3, 6.4).
#
# M7 shipped detection with no trigger of its own and said why: periodic
# detection is M10's deliverable by name. This is that trigger, in the only
# shape the repo permits — SPEC 6.3 forbids continuous high-frequency
# polling and names a low-frequency timer as the preferred alternative, and
# punard-reconcile.timer (M4 §5) already litigated timer-vs-daemon-thread
# and chose the timer.
#
# WHY 240 s, and why it is not "a second timer" in the honest sense:
#   - It is an exact multiple of the shipping 120 s reconcile period, and
#     AccuracySec=30 lets systemd COALESCE the two into the same wakeup. In
#     the steady state M10 therefore adds no new wakeup INSTANT at all —
#     only a slightly longer one every other reconcile tick.
#   - Worst case sighting-to-alert is 240 + 30 s, which is stated on the
#     surface (`punarctl agents list`'s footer) rather than rounded down.
#   - Faster (60 s) would quadruple wakeups and change no decision a human
#     or an administrator makes: M10 is not armed (law 4), so a detection
#     four minutes old and one sixty seconds old are acted on identically.
#   - Slower (900 s) would routinely miss agent runs that live ten minutes
#     — a realistic shape for a coding agent — and would make the honest
#     limitation larger than the feature.
#
# The timer is deliberately NOT stopped for the idle-RAM sampling window:
# budgets are measured against the shipping configuration, and 240 s is half
# the frequency of something that window has contained since M4.
#
# The honest limitation, printed on the CLI surface and stated here because
# a unit file is where a reader looks for the cadence: a process that starts
# and exits inside one interval, and touches nothing Punar mediates, is
# never seen. Sampling detection has that hole by construction; closing it
# needs exec-time notification, which is exactly the broad tracing SPEC 1.14
# rules out. It is stated, not engineered around.
#
# Enabled statically via a VENDOR timers.target.wants symlink in the extra
# tree — mkosi applies Arch's presets to /etc after extra trees, so /etc
# enablements and postinst systemctl are preset-wiped (the twice-verified M1
# lesson; punard-reconcile.timer's precedent). `systemctl is-enabled`
# reports `disabled` for a /usr/lib .wants unit, so m10-check asserts the
# SYMLINK plus `Wants=` in `systemctl show timers.target`, never is-enabled
# (the M4 lesson).

[Unit]
Description=Punar periodic shadow-AI detection pass

[Timer]
OnBootSec=240
OnUnitActiveSec=240
AccuracySec=30

[Install]
WantedBy=timers.target
